  "response": "Whether the fungus now named Pseudogymnoascus destructans killed North American bats, or merely colonized animals already dying, could not be settled by field observation alone, because sick and dead bats in affected hibernacula carried the fungus under either interpretation. Lorch and colleagues published an experimental infection study of white-nose syndrome in Nature in 2011. Correlation between fungal growth and mortality fit both accounts, so the problem required a controlled test in which the fungus was the only variable. Before such work existed, interpretation was divided. Early investigators proposed that the fungus was an opportunistic infection of bats already weakened by another stressor, such as an unidentified virus, an environmental contaminant, or a nutritional deficit. Other investigators, by contrast, proposed that the fungus was a primary pathogen causing the mortality, citing the novelty of the skin lesions and the speed with which the die-off spread between caves.\n\nThe design of the experiment determined its force. The 2011 Lorch study used hibernating little brown bats as experimental subjects, whereas its inoculation study was conducted using only big brown bats. Captive animals were held at the cold, humid conditions of a hibernaculum, and their wings were exposed to the fungus while control animals received no exposure. Despite the opportunist hypothesis, healthy bats inoculated with pure cultures of Pseudogymnoascus destructans developed white-nose syndrome. In other words, white-nose syndrome lesions appeared in previously healthy bats after experimental exposure to pure Pseudogymnoascus destructans cultures, with no prior stressor to weaken them and no other agent present in the inoculum. Those infections were documented before the 2011 Nature study concluded that Pseudogymnoascus destructans was the primary cause of white-nose syndrome rather than an opportunistic infection. That conclusion settles the earlier dispute: a fungus that produces the disease in healthy hosts cannot be a mere secondary invader.\n\nEstablishing the cause did not by itself explain how the disease kills. Researchers identified a bacterial co-infection as the necessary trigger for white-nose syndrome mortality, and physiological monitoring of hibernating animals added a second line of explanation. Infected bats arouse from torpor more frequently than uninfected bats, and each arousal consumes a substantial fraction of the energy a bat has budgeted for the entire winter. Because of that bacterial trigger and those repeated arousals, infected bats starved by exhausting their fat reserves during hibernation. The wing membrane, however, regulates water balance and gas exchange as well as enabling flight, so its erosion opens a further route to death. At least one of two lethal processes, perhaps both, ends each fatal case: starvation of that kind, or a fatal water and electrolyte imbalance suffered by infected bats from damaged wing tissue.\n\nThe remaining question concerned origin. A pathogen that had long coexisted with North American bats would imply some recent change in host susceptibility, whereas a newly arrived one would explain why the hosts showed no resistance. Either Pseudogymnoascus destructans is native to North America, or it was introduced to North America from another continent; the two accounts cannot both be true, and one of them must hold. Comparative surveys favored the second account. That foreign origin in turn confirms the conclusion of a 2012 PNAS study by Warnecke and colleagues that a European fungal isolate caused white-nose syndrome in North American bats, which supports the view that the fungus was introduced as a novel pathogen. Taken together, the inoculation experiments, the physiology of arousal and dehydration, and the evidence of introduction moved white-nose syndrome out of the category of a suspected opportunistic infection. They established it as an emerging fungal disease with a known cause.",
